From 4ed2719d7bf49fdadb00ed46cb392ce84153fa19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 10:25:16 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] rdna-boosts: block 14: relaxed-tensor split mode
 (TEMPORARY: MoE amax per-device scalar)

This block is a LOCAL, TEMPORARY relaxation of upstream behaviour, not a
feature of the delivery.  It exists so the MoE amax arches (flint, mellum,
mistral4) can run -sm tensor today; it is expected to be replaced once
upstream lands a real fix, and it can be dropped without touching any other
block.  Limitations are recorded in patches/README.md (block 14) and
GREEDY-PURITY.md; do not treat `-sm relaxed-tensor` as a supported mode.

Why upstream refuses today: #30861/#31081 -- the MoE amax rescale pools a
whole matmul row (`pool_1d(abs(cur), MAX, k = s = n_ff_exp)`) to divide `cur`
by a power of two and undo it on the down-projection output.  Under
`-sm tensor` no device holds a complete row, so each one can only compute the
max of its own slice.  #31081 removed the Meta POOL_1D case (which claimed
MIRRORED, a false claim) and denied -sm tensor for those three arches.

Add a split mode instead of a blanket deny.  `-sm relaxed-tensor` places the
weights exactly like `-sm tensor` (same Meta wrapper, FA required, expert
tables split: 0 extra VRAM) but also admits a replicated tensor whose values
differ per device:

  - ggml: new GGML_BACKEND_SPLIT_AXIS_PER_DEVICE = 12 -- same shape/positions
    everywhere, per-device values.  `handle_pool_1d` produces it for an
    axis-0 split (whole-row MAX only), `handle_bin_bcast` lets it be consumed
    elementwise and broadcast, and every copy/gather/read-back path aborts with
    a message instead of silently returning one device's value.

  - The delayed-reduce MUL-chain in graph_compute accepts it, so the reduce
    point stays AFTER the per-device undo: each device applies its own `e` to
    its own partial and only then is the sum taken.  That is what makes the
    per-device value cancel (`e` is a power of two, so the scaling commutes
    with the sum), and `e_local <= e_global` means every device keeps at least
    the reference's f16 headroom.

  - llama: LLAMA_SPLIT_MODE_RELAXED_TENSOR = 4, `-sm relaxed-tensor`; every
    "is this the tensor path?" site goes through llm_split_mode_is_tensor_like().
    `-sm tensor` keeps upstream's strict behaviour and the deny, but the error
    now points at the new mode.

Measured (Flint Q8_0, 2x R9700): greedy text byte-identical to `-sm layer`
and to itself at 3 GPUs; with the amax divisor forced to 1 (so `e` != 1) the
POOL_1D pooled max differs across devices on ~half of the ~185 per-run calls
and the text still matches `-sm layer` exactly.  A non-amax arch (Qwen3.5-4B)
is byte-identical under tensor and relaxed-tensor.  `ggml_pool_1d` has exactly
one producer in the graph, so the relaxed state is unreachable for any other
arch.

NOT claimed: bit-identity to a single device on a backend whose in-kernel
src1 rescale is non-linear (Vulkan's clamp; RDNA/HIP is exact), any behaviour
under -ncmoe (the expert arena still refuses tensor-like modes), and
mellum/mistral4 at all (enabled by the same change, unvalidated -- no model).

Re-based onto a691b2345 (r236): **zero conflicts** (the survey's prediction), and no drift edits
needed.  Exit verified on this tree (gfx1201, ROCm 10.1.0, VMM build):

* `-sm tensor` still refuses flint with this block's message
  ("LLAMA_SPLIT_MODE_TENSOR not implemented for architecture 'flint'; this arch's MoE amax rescale needs
  '-sm relaxed-tensor'"), i.e. upstream's #31081 deny is intact and the block only adds the escape hatch.
* RecursiveAI/Flint Q8_0, 3 GPU, `-sm relaxed-tensor` loads and generates, and its greedy text is
  **identical to `-sm layer`** (904c62d1d4f9, 169 chars) -- the block's own claim, on the arch it was
  validated for.  (mellum/mistral4 remain unvalidated here: no model, as the block says.)
* The mode is a no-op for a non-amax arch: 35B-A3B Q8_0 3 GPU `-sm relaxed-tensor` == `-sm tensor`
  (86b7f9b5aa80).
* Coherence unchanged against R14: dense 4B 1de99b7e6ee6, 30B-A3B 4b4d56191368, Flash-Next GSQ
  7a8734a9f1a6 (the ggml-backend-meta and model-graph hunks touch the tensor-split path, so the
  Flash-Next arm matters here).
